# Test integrity

Do not optimize for test passage. Optimize for the behavior the tests are
intended to verify.

- Never add a special case, hardcoded value, test-environment branch, shim, or
  workaround to satisfy an observed test result.
- Never make the test agree with the code instead: no expected value changed to
  the observed one, no loosened assertion, no `skip`/`xfail`, no dropped case,
  no mocking the unit under test.
- When a test failure drives a change, implement the general invariant rather
  than the failing case, and state a justification that stays true and complete
  if the test file is deleted.
- Stop and report when the only way to pass conflicts with the public contract
  or the surrounding design. Do not force the suite green.

Generalizing to an invariant is not authorization to broaden scope or add
speculative defensive branches.

The `test-integrity` skill carries the diagnosis vocabulary, the durable-trace
check, and the reporting contract. Load it when a test failure is driving a
change or when reviewing one.

